How to read it. Each bar is a stock’s expected-return estimate, split into the factor part (blue) and the alpha component (gold); dots mark the total. In the table (bp a month), bold cells are each model’s own alpha. Source: illustrative numbers, not the paper’s data; the layout follows the paper’s Table 1.



What to notice. Compared with Reset, B’s own alpha (bottom-right) shrinks in both stories. In Story 1 the top-right cell drops: B’s estimate is easier for any factor to explain. In Story 2 the bottom-left cell drops: B’s factor explains A’s estimate better. Only the crossing tells you which story you are in. In the paper’s data, the real table looks like Story 1 (Table 1, p. 13). Dragging further, B’s own alpha hits zero when its factor bends as far as its estimate keeps the tilt, then rises again. The toy has one factor and five equally weighted stocks, and B’s estimate only scales down A’s tilt; in the paper the estimate also changes direction, not only scale (p. 14).

