This illustrative figure is computed in the browser and needs JavaScript. At its starting values (ρ = 0.6; a share of 0.75 of the loading’s weight on earnings-to-price), the loading reproduces 57.6% of the book-to-market position; after the plain-ruler hedge, 26.5% of the hedged position still lies on the loading, and after the characteristic-ruler hedge, 0.0%.



How to read it. Coverage is the share of the book-to-market position, by squared size under the characteristic ruler, that the loading reproduces. The two hedge bars give the share of each hedged position that still lies on the loading. The scale-up in the readout is 1 ÷ (1 − coverage): the paper’s position-size identity with its second term, the shape of the remaining stock weights, set to one (eq. 12, p. 28). Source: illustrative numbers computed in the browser from equations 2, 10 and 11 of the paper; no data file.


In the paper’s data the gap is large. In the free-intercept, 153-characteristic model, the plain-ruler hedge leaves on average 46.10% of a book-to-market position on the model’s loadings, 53.51% of a momentum position and 65.13% of a larger-firm size position; the characteristic-ruler hedge leaves 0.00% (Table IA.2, p. 63). Figure 4 below switches between the two.

